<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Snowflake on System Design</title><link>https://system-design.devops-monk.com/tags/snowflake/</link><description>Recent content in Snowflake on System Design</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Sun, 31 May 2026 10:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://system-design.devops-monk.com/tags/snowflake/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Design a Unique ID Generator in Distributed Systems</title><link>https://system-design.devops-monk.com/2026/05/design-unique-id-generator/</link><pubDate>Sun, 31 May 2026 10:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://system-design.devops-monk.com/2026/05/design-unique-id-generator/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Every row in your database needs a name. For years that name came from one line of SQL:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#d8dee9;background-color:#2e3440;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-sql" data-lang="sql"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#81a1c1;font-weight:bold"&gt;CREATE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#81a1c1;font-weight:bold"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt; orders &lt;span style="color:#eceff4"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; id &lt;span style="color:#81a1c1"&gt;BIGINT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#81a1c1;font-weight:bold"&gt;PRIMARY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#81a1c1;font-weight:bold"&gt;KEY&lt;/span&gt; AUTO_INCREMENT&lt;span style="color:#eceff4"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#eceff4"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#eceff4"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;The database hands out 1, 2, 3, 4. They are unique, they are sortable, they are small. It is a solved problem — right up until the moment you have &lt;strong&gt;two&lt;/strong&gt; databases.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Then it stops working, quietly and catastrophically. Both databases happily hand out ID &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;. Two different orders, same identifier. Your foreign keys now point at the wrong rows, and no error was raised anywhere.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>